.TH "UBERTOOTH\-BTLE" "1" "October 2026" "Project Ubertooth" "User Commands"
.SH "NAME"
\fBubertooth\-btle\fR \- Bluetooth Low Energy (BLE) sniffing and more
.SH "SYNOPSIS"
.nf
ubertooth\-btle \-f [\-A 37|38|39] [\-r output\.pcapng]
.fi
.SH "DESCRIPTION"
\fBubertooth\-btle\fR is a tool for doing Fun Stuff(TM) with BLE\. It can do the following things:
.IP "\(bu" 4
Sniff connections
.IP "\(bu" 4
Interfere with connections
.IP "\(bu" 4
Send advertising packets (experimental)
.IP "" 0
.P
Sniffing connections is the most robust feature supported by \fBubertooth\-btle\fR\. It has two primary modes of operation: follow mode and promiscuous mode\.
.P
Follow mode is the preferred mode for general use\. In this mode, Ubertooth will listen on one of three advertising channels waiting for a BLE connection to be established\. When a connection is established, Ubertooth will hop along the data channels, passively capturing the data sent between the central and peripheral\. After the connection terminates, Ubertooth will return to the advertising channel and wait for another connection\.
.P
No\-follow mode is similar to follow mode, but it only logs advertising packets and will not follow connections as they are established\.
.P
Promiscuous mode is an experimental mode for sniffing connections after they have already been established\. This mode can be used to sniff long\-lived connections\.
.P
When sniffing, Ubertooth can only operate in either follow mode or promiscuous mode, but not both at the same time\. If you are unsure which mode to use, use follow mode\.
.P
While promiscuous mode hunts for an access address it is gated by the board's squelch; follow and no\-follow mode are not\. \fBubertooth\-btle\fR does not set the squelch, so it is the default \-120 dBm unless \fBubertooth\-util \-z\fR set another value while the board was idle, and the firmware resets it on every mode change, so it never carries over from an earlier capture\. See ubertooth\-util(1), SQUELCH\.
.P
By default, Ubertooth will follow any connection it observes\. You can limit this to following a specific Bluetooth Address (BD ADDR) using the \fB\-t\fR command line flag\. \fB\-t\fR is for follow mode: it picks out a connection by the device address its advertising and connect packets carry, which is what \fB\-f\fR watches for (in \fB\-n\fR the board drops the advertising packets that do not match it, and nothing else)\. Promiscuous mode starts on a connection that is already running, whose packets carry no device address at all, so \fB\-t\fR has nothing to match there and is refused with \fB\-p\fR\. For example, the following command will only sniff connections where the central or peripheral's BD ADDR is \fB22:44:66:88:AA:CC\fR:
.IP "" 4
.nf
ubertooth\-btle \-f \-t 22:44:66:88:AA:CC
.fi
.IP "" 0
.P
\fB\-t\fR can also take a mask length in CIDR\-like notation\. Masks can be between 1 and 48 bits long, with a 48 bit mask matching the entire address\. Using a /24 mask will filter on just the OUI\. For example, to limit sniffing to just TI devices with the OUI 00:1A:7D, use the following:
.IP "" 4
.nf
ubertooth\-btle \-t 00:1A:7D:00:00:00/24
.fi
.IP "" 0
.P
Filters persist until they are explicitly cleared or the system restarts (either via \fBubertooth\-util \-r\fR or unplug/replug)\. To clear a filter, use the special filter \fBnone\fR\. Example:
.IP "" 4
.nf
ubertooth\-btle \-t none
.fi
.IP "" 0
.P
When filtering, previous versions of the firmware would still log all advertising packets but only follow connections based on the filter parameters\. As of 2018\-06\-R1, advertising packets that do not match the filter are dropped\.
.P
In all sniffing modes, Ubertooth can log data to PCAP or PcapNG with a variety of pseudoheaders\. The recommended logging format is PcapNG (\fB\-r\fR) or PCAP with LE Pseudoheader (\fB\-q\fR)\. For compatibility with crackle (see \fIUSING WITH CRACKLE\fR), use PCAP with PPI (\fB\-c\fR)\.
.P
In every sniffing mode, a packet carrying a Bluetooth 4\.2 Data Length Extension payload, up to its 251\-byte maximum, is captured whole: on the screen, in PCAP and in PCAPNG\. The board sends such a packet in several fixed\-size records, which are put back together before anything is decoded or written\. Older firmware cut every packet to 50 bytes\.
.P
Interfering with connections is a feature for causing intentional interference with newly established or long\-lived connections\. When this attack succeeds, the BLE connection between the central and peripheral will be terminated\. Pair the \fB\-i\fR or \fB\-I\fR flag with \fB\-f\fR to interfere with new connections or \fB\-p\fR to interfere with long\-lived connections\. \fB\-i\fR or \fB\-I\fR (or their older spellings \fB\-j\fR and \fB\-J\fR) with neither \fB\-f\fR nor \fB\-p\fR is refused before any device is opened: there is no connection to interfere with\. This describes the feature as it is meant to work; no capture mode acts on it at the moment (see KNOWN BROKEN FEATURES)\. Note that causing intentional interference may be illegal in your jurisdiction\. Check your local laws before using this feature\.
.P
Finally, \fBubertooth\-btle\fR supports transmitting advertising packets with a specified BD ADDR\. This feature, referred to as faux slave mode, is experimental and may not function as intended\. Use at your own risk\.
.SH "EXAMPLES"
Sniff all connections on advertising channel 38, logging all data to PcapNG:
.IP "" 4
.nf
ubertooth\-btle \-f \-A 38 \-r log\.pcapng
.fi
.IP "" 0
.P
Log advertising packets without following connections:
.IP "" 4
.nf
ubertooth\-btle \-n
.fi
.IP "" 0
.P
Interfere with connections recovered with promiscuous mode:
.IP "" 4
.nf
ubertooth\-btle \-p \-I
.fi
.IP "" 0
.P
Send advertising packets using BD ADDR \fB22:44:66:88:AA:CC\fR:
.IP "" 4
.nf
ubertooth\-btle \-s 22:44:66:88:AA:CC
.fi
.IP "" 0
.SH "OPTIONS"
.TP
\fB\-h\fR
Displays help message
.TP
\fB\-V\fR
print the version: the host tools, then the firmware version and build of each attached board; see ubertooth(7), COMMON OPTIONS
.P
Major modes:
.TP
\fB\-f\fR
Follow mode: sniff connections as they are established
.TP
\fB\-n\fR
No\-follow mode: log advertising packets but don't follow connections
.TP
\fB\-p\fR
Promiscuous mode: sniff already\-established connections\. It sets no channel of its own: it listens on the one the board is holding, 2440 MHz unless \fBubertooth\-util \-c\fR left it on another even frequency\. It discovers the access address of a connection on that channel, unless an earlier \fBubertooth\-btle \-a <address>\fR command gave the board one to follow; nothing else makes it skip discovery, and \fB\-t\fR is refused with it\. As it works a connection out it reports each parameter it has recovered: the access address, the CRC init value, the hop interval and the hop increment\. Those lines go to standard output, or to standard error once \fB\-q \-\fR or \fB\-c \-\fR has taken standard output, so a capture written to a file still says which connection it is of\.
.IP
The hop interval is not read off the air; it is worked out by timing how long the connection takes to come back to the channel being listened on\. That needs several of those returns in a row, so it arrives well after the CRC init value: about ten seconds on a 30 ms connection whose returns are all heard, and proportionally longer on a slower one\. It is reported only once the spacings repeat and give a figure inside the range a connection interval may take, so a connection the board hears only part of gives the access address and the CRC init value and no interval or increment\.
.IP
Three things it does not report an interval for\. A connection using fewer than about five data channels comes back too often for its pattern to fit in what the board keeps\. A connection event that runs on for more than one interval, which a long exchange does, puts sightings inside it that are not returns\. And a board that hears the returns unevenly, as a capture losing packets does, never sees a pattern repeat\.
.IP
One case it cannot see through: a board that hears exactly every second return, every time, reports twice the real interval, because two returns of one connection and one return of a connection twice as slow are the same span\.
.IP
Nothing hops until the interval is known, so a capture up to that point holds the one channel\.
.TP
\fB\-s <BD ADDR>\fR
Inject advertising packets using specified BD ADDR\. Like \fB\-a\fR, it does not go with \fB\-f\fR, \fB\-n\fR or \fB\-p\fR\. The value is one whole address and nothing else: a faux slave advertises from a single address, so the \fB/<bits>\fR mask and \fBnone\fR that \fB\-t\fR takes are refused here, each as the address it is not (\fBerror: faux slave address must be xx:xx:xx:xx:xx:xx (got none)\fR)\. Both used to be parsed and then dropped, so \fB\-s none\fR advertised from \fB00:00:00:00:00:00\fR\.
.P
Interference (only with \fB\-f\fR or \fB\-p\fR):
.TP
\fB\-i\fR
Interfere with one connection and return to idle\. Accepted but inert at the moment; see KNOWN BROKEN FEATURES
.TP
\fB\-I\fR
Interfere continuously with many connections\. Accepted but inert at the moment; see KNOWN BROKEN FEATURES
.TP
\fB\-j\fR
Original "jam" spelling of \fB\-i\fR; does exactly what \fB\-i\fR does
.TP
\fB\-J\fR
Original "jam" spelling of \fB\-I\fR; does exactly what \fB\-I\fR does
.P
Filtering:
.TP
\fB\-t <BD ADDR>\fR
Limit connection following and interference in follow mode to the specified BD ADDR\. It is for the follow modes: \fB\-f\fR follows only a connection whose advertising or connect packets carry this address, and \fB\-n\fR drops the advertising packets that do not\. Every form of it, \fB\-t none\fR included, is refused with \fB\-p\fR, which has no device address to match against: \fBerror: \-t does not go with \-p: promiscuous mode has no device address to match a target against\fR\. The combination used to be accepted, and the board then read "a target was set" as "the host gave an access address", so \fB\-p\fR followed \fB8e89bed6\fR and captured nothing\. \fB\-s\fR and \fB\-t\fR are two different addresses and a command can give both: \fB\-s A \-t B\fR advertises from A and follows B, and \fB\-t none\fR clears the target without touching the address \fB\-s\fR was given\. They shared one address, so whichever of the two was written last used to set both, and \fB\-s <BD ADDR> \-t none\fR advertised from \fB00:00:00:00:00:00\fR without a word\.
.P
Logging:
.TP
\fB\-r <output\.pcapng>\fR
Log to PcapNG (preferred)\. PcapNG output cannot be streamed to stdout because the writer must seek while finalizing the file; \fB\-r \-\fR is rejected\.
.TP
\fB\-q <output\.pcap|\->\fR
Log to PCAP with \fBDLT_BLUETOOTH_LE_LL_WITH_PHDR\fR\. Use \fB\-\fR to stream binary PCAP to stdout\.
.TP
\fB\-c <output\.pcap|\->\fR
Log to PCAP with PPI (for compatibility with crackle(1))\. Use \fB\-\fR to stream binary PCAP to stdout, for example:
.IP "" 4
.nf
ubertooth\-btle \-f \-c \- | wireshark \-k \-i \-
.fi
.IP "" 0
.IP
When stdout capture is selected, regular packet/status text is suppressed so it cannot corrupt the binary stream; errors remain on stderr\. Such a run does not produce the per\-packet dump at all, so the decoder's complaints about an advertising field it could not read in one of those packets do not reach stderr either\. The lines that answer an option in the same command go to stderr rather than being suppressed with it: the \fBAccess address:\fR line of \fB\-a\fR, the \fBCRC:\fR line of \fB\-k\fR, the target lines of \fB\-t\fR, and the connection parameters \fB\-p\fR recovers\. The tool refuses to write binary capture data directly to an interactive terminal: \fBerror: stdout is a tty, refusing to write binary capture data to it\fR, with exit status 1 and before any device is opened\.
.P
The times in a logged capture follow the board's capture clock, which starts over every 429\.50 s in the \fB\-f\fR and \fB\-n\fR modes and every 327\.68 s in promiscuous mode\. A promiscuous packet carries a count of how often it has; the packets of the other two modes carry none, so how often it started over is worked out from the host's own clock instead: the time the host saw pass between two packets says how many periods lie between their clock readings, so a quiet gap keeps its time however long it was\. Before, a gap longer than one period left every later time short by a period, since the clock then reads higher than it did and nothing in the capture shows it started over at all\.
.P
Times that still come out short are reported, on standard error: \fBwarning: packet times are behind the host clock by more than half the capture clock's 429\.50 s period: a quiet gap lost a clock wrap, or the host clock was stepped forwards\fR\. Both causes look the same from here, which is why the message names each\.
.P
Miscellaneous:
.TP
\fB\-A <37|38|39>\fR
Which advertising channel to use in follow mode (\fB\-f\fR, \fB\-n\fR) and faux slave mode (\fB\-s\fR) (default: 37)\. Refused with \fB\-p\fR, which sets no channel of its own\.
.TP
\fB\-a [address]\fR
Get (\fB\-a\fR) or set (\fB\-a <address>\fR) the access address used in promiscuous mode\. The address is 1 to 8 hex digits (\fB\-a 8e89bed6\fR); fewer than 8 means leading zeros (\fB\-a 12345\fR sets \fB00012345\fR)\. Anything else is refused before the device is opened\. Getting and setting in one command is refused; other options such as \fB\-k\fR still run after a get\. \fB\-a\fR does not go with a sniffing mode (\fB\-f\fR, \fB\-n\fR, \fB\-p\fR), which runs until it is interrupted; the combination is refused before any device is opened\. So an address for \fB\-p\fR is given by a command of its own first: \fBubertooth\-btle \-a <address>\fR and then \fBubertooth\-btle \-p\fR\. It is the one setting that makes \fB\-p\fR skip discovery, and the board keeps it until its low\-energy state next resets: a connection timing out during a \fB\-p\fR run, a \fB\-p\fR run that went looking for an address itself, \fBubertooth\-util \-r\fR, or a re\-plug\. A follow run (\fB\-f\fR, \fB\-n\fR) does not clear it\. Promiscuous mode then discovers an address again rather than falling back to the advertising one\.
.TP
\fB\-k [01]\fR
Read (\fB\-k\fR) or set (\fB\-k 0\fR or \fB\-k 1\fR) CRC verification (default: 0)\. Any other value is refused\. A set and a read in one command both happen, the set first, so the read reports the new value, in whichever order they are written; two sets are refused, as two values for one setting are everywhere (ubertooth(7), OPTION VALUES)\. In a sniffing mode both run once the mode has started, because entering a mode clears the setting, and the firmware then turns verification on and off itself as it recovers a connection\.
.TP
\fB\-v [01]\fR
The older spelling of \fB\-k\fR, \fBdeprecated\fR\. It still does what \fB\-k\fR does and prints one line on standard error \-\- \fBwarning: \-v for the CRC verify setting is deprecated and will be removed; use \-k\fR \-\- so a script that uses it keeps working for this release\. After that \fB\-v\fR means verbose here as it does in every other tool (ubertooth(7), COMMON OPTIONS)\. A command line that gives both spellings names one setting twice and is refused: \fBerror: \-k cannot be combined with \-v; they are the same CRC verify setting written two ways\fR\.
.TP
\fB\-x <0\-32>\fR
Allow n access address violations (default: 32)\. Filtering occurs on host\.
.P
Data source:
.TP
\fB\-U <index|serial>\fR
select an Ubertooth by enumeration index or serial number; see ubertooth(7), SELECTING A DEVICE
.SH "KNOWN BROKEN FEATURES"
Interference (\fB\-i\fR, \fB\-I\fR, and their original jam spellings \fB\-j\fR and \fB\-J\fR) is accepted, but no capture mode acts on it yet: the board follows or listens to the connection as usual and transmits nothing, so a run with one of these options is no different from a run without it\. The tool prints one \fBwarning:\fR line saying so when any of them is given\. The DESCRIPTION and OPTIONS below describe the feature as it is meant to work; making it work again is tracked in issue #871 \fIhttps://github\.com/SqueakyFloors/ubertooth/issues/871\fR\.
.SH "USING WITH CRACKLE"
\fBcrackle\fR is a tool for cracking the BLE key exchange and decrypting encrypted data\. To capture data for use with \fBcrackle\fR, sniff connections in follow mode using \fB\-f\fR and log data to PCAP/PPI using \fB\-c\fR\. Example:
.IP "" 4
.nf
ubertooth\-btle \-f \-c crack\.pcap
.fi
.IP "" 0
.P
Refer to \fBcrackle\fR documentation for further details\.
.SH "TRANSMIT POWER"
Injecting advertising packets (\fB\-s\fR) transmits at the CC2400 power level set with \fBubertooth\-util \-a\fR and leaves it unchanged\. A board that has not been given a level transmits at 7 (0 dBm), the CC2400's reset value\. Sniffing does not transmit and does not change the level\. Interference (\fB\-i\fR, \fB\-I\fR, \fB\-j\fR, \fB\-J\fR) would transmit at the same level, but no capture mode acts on it at the moment (see KNOWN BROKEN FEATURES), so it transmits nothing\. These levels are at the CC2400 radio; the Ubertooth One's RF amplifier changes the actual output after that\.
.SH "ARGUMENTS"
\fBubertooth\-btle\fR takes options only\. A word that is not an option, including a bare \fB\-\fR, is refused before any device is opened and named: \fBerror: unexpected argument '<word>'\fR, with exit status 1\.
.P
\fB\-h\fR and \fB\-V\fR end the command only once the whole command line has parsed, so a stray word or a bad option value is reported even alongside them\. Both answer on standard output, so a command line with both prints both, the usage text last; the order they were written in makes no difference\.
.P
An option value must be a whole value in its range, or it is refused the same way instead of being read as 0, for example \fBerror: advertising channel index must be 37\-39 (got 40)\fR\.
.P
A bare \fB\-\fR is still the stdin/stdout value of \fB\-q\fR, \fB\-c\fR and \fB\-r\fR\. See ubertooth(7), OPTION VALUES, for the forms an option's value can take, including the optional values of \fB\-a\fR and \fB\-k\fR\.
.P
An option combination the tool cannot carry out is refused there too, before any device is opened, so the message never depends on which boards are attached: more than one of \fB\-f\fR, \fB\-n\fR and \fB\-p\fR gives \fBerror: choose one of \-f, \-n or \-p\fR, and a read and a set of the access address in one command gives \fBerror: \-a cannot both read and set the access address\fR\.
.P
\fB\-a\fR or \fB\-s\fR alongside a sniffing mode gives \fBerror: \-a and \-s do not go with \-f, \-n or \-p: a sniffing mode runs until it is interrupted\fR, so there is nothing for an access address or faux slave mode request to run after\. \fB\-t\fR and \fB\-k\fR are not in that list: \fB\-t\fR is sent before the mode starts, which is what it is for, and the \fB\-k\fR options run once it has started\.
.P
\fB\-t\fR with \fB\-p\fR gives \fBerror: \-t does not go with \-p: promiscuous mode has no device address to match a target against\fR, whatever the target is: a connection that is already running names no device, so \fB\-t none\fR is refused beside \fB\-p\fR as well\.
.P
\fB\-i\fR or \fB\-I\fR without \fB\-f\fR or \fB\-p\fR gives \fBerror: \-i needs \-f or \-p: there is no connection to interfere with otherwise\fR, naming the spelling that was used (\fB\-j\fR and \fB\-J\fR included)\.
.P
That includes \fB\-i\fR on its own: it says more than listing every major mode would, because only two of them can carry interference\.
.P
A command line with no work in it is refused the same way\. \fB\-U\fR, \fB\-A\fR, \fB\-x\fR and the capture options only shape work another option asks for, so a command made up of those alone, or a bare \fBubertooth\-btle\fR, gives \fBerror: nothing to do: choose a major mode (\-f, \-n or \-p), or \-a, \-k, \-s or \-t\fR, on standard error with nothing on standard output\. A bare \fBubertooth\-btle\fR is the one refusal the usage text follows, also on standard error; every other refused command line is the \fBerror:\fR line alone, and \fB\-h\fR prints the usage text on standard output\.
.P
A refused command line creates and truncates nothing: \fB\-r\fR, \fB\-q\fR and \fB\-c\fR name their file while the command line is read, and it is opened only once every check has passed\. The board is opened and its firmware is checked before that, so a run refused for either reason leaves no empty file behind either\.
.SH "DIAGNOSTICS"
Standard output carries only what was asked for: either the decoded packets or the capture data of \fB\-q \-\fR or \fB\-c \-\fR, plus the lines that answer \fB\-a\fR, \fB\-k\fR and \fB\-t\fR and the parameters \fB\-p\fR recovers\. With the capture on standard output, standard output belongs to the capture for the whole run: those lines move to standard error, and the decoded packets are not printed at all\.
.P
The \fBrssi=\fR figure on each packet's \fBsystime=\fR line is that packet's own level in dBm: the strongest RSSI reading the board reports for the packet, through a piecewise\-linear model of the CC2400 datasheet's RSSI curve\. It is the same number the signal field of a \fB\-q\fR or \fB\-r\fR capture carries for that packet, so a dump and a capture of one run agree\. A \fB\-c\fR PPI capture carries the board's raw counts instead, which are not dBm\. The model has ends, so \fBrssi=0\fR means "at least this loud" and \fBrssi=\-120\fR means "no louder than this"\.
.P
Earlier releases printed the weakest reading the board reports for the packet instead, through a rougher approximation\. That figure ran far below the packet's own level: on the bench it reached \-96 dBm where the quietest packet the capture recorded was \-78, and a dump and a capture of the same run disagreed by as much as 90 dB on an individual packet\.
.P
Errors, warnings and progress go to standard error, each error as one line starting \fBerror:\fR, for example \fBerror: USB poll failed\fR when the board stops answering during a capture, \fBerror: jamming not supported by this firmware\fR, \fBerror: target set failed\fR, or \fBerror: target address must be xx:xx:xx:xx:xx:xx (got 11:22:33:44:55)\fR\. A value that is not an address reads the same way whatever is wrong with it, and names the address the option sets, so \fB\-s\fR gives \fBerror: faux slave address must be xx:xx:xx:xx:xx:xx (got <word>)\fR, including for the \fBnone\fR and \fB22:44:66:88:aa:cc/24\fR that \fB\-t\fR takes, and a bad target mask gives \fBerror: target mask must be 0\-48 (got 49)\fR\. A capture file that cannot be created is reported as \fBerror: <file>: <reason>\fR\.
.P
A capture that is losing packets says so\. This tool reads its packets one at a time, and a reply that held part of a record cannot be read as one, so it is dropped and reported once per run: \fBwarning: a poll reply arrived with 32 of a packet's 64 bytes and was dropped; the capture is losing packets\fR\. The capture runs on and ends as it otherwise would, so the warning is the only sign\.
.P
Packets the board had to throw away are reported too\. It holds captured packets in a queue until this tool reads them, and a queue with no room left loses the next packet; a packet longer than 50 bytes takes several of the queue's slots and is lost whole, so a busy capture can lose the long packets while the short ones still arrive\. The first time it happens the capture warns \fBwarning: the board's packet queue filled up and it dropped at least one packet; this capture has a gap\fR, and when the capture ends it says how many such gaps there were: \fBwarning: the capture has 3 gaps where the board's queue filled up and it dropped packets\fR\. The count is of gaps, not of lost packets: one full queue can swallow several packets, and the board sends no number\. Nothing says which packets are missing, so a capture with gaps is complete only between them\. The board reports a gap on the first packet that gets through after it, so a capture that ends in the moment after a gap does not report that last one\.
.P
A capture transfer error is reported the same way\. The chip's transfer controller can report an error on the channel a capture receives into: what the board was receiving at the time had arrived, so it is kept and marked, while some of its bytes may be wrong and whatever the fault cut short before it is lost\. The first mark warns \fBwarning: the board had a transfer error while capturing; what it was receiving was kept, but some of those bytes may be wrong and the capture may be missing packets around it\fR, and when the capture ends it says how many there were: \fBwarning: the capture has 3 transfer errors where a packet may be wrong or missing\fR\. ubertooth\-util(1) \fB\-w\fR reports the board's own count of them since power\-on, over every capture the board has run\.
.P
Symbols the board wrote over are reported the same way\. The board receives into one block of symbols while its capture loop reads the one before it, and when a block finishes arriving before the loop has read that one, the new symbols go over the old ones\. The block is gone before anything looked in it for a packet, so this loss has no packet to point at and nothing in the output marks where it happened\. The first one warns \fBwarning: the board fell behind while capturing and wrote new symbols over a block it had not read yet; whatever was in the air then is missing from this capture\fR, and when the capture ends it says how many there were: \fBwarning: the capture is missing 3 blocks of symbols the board wrote over before reading\fR\.
.P
A poll the board does not answer is tried again, up to three attempts, before the capture gives up: a board busy elsewhere for a moment no longer ends a capture that has been running for hours\. A board that has left the bus ends it at once\. The retry saves the capture, not the packet: the board takes a packet out of its queue as it answers the poll, so the one a timed\-out poll was carrying is gone\.
.P
Each request that sets a mode up is named on its own, so the line says which one the board refused: \fBerror: could not set the advertising channel\fR, \fBerror: could not set the advertising data\fR, \fBerror: could not start advertising\fR for \fB\-s\fR, and \fBerror: could not start follow mode\fR (or \fBno\-follow\fR, or \fBpromiscuous\fR) for the sniffing modes\. No capture starts after one of these\.
.P
\fB\-q\fR and \fB\-c\fR share one capture handle, so a second file between them is ignored with \fBwarning: ignoring extra capture file: <name>\fR and the first one given is used; \fB\-r\fR has its own handle, so \fB\-q a\.pcap \-r b\.pcapng\fR writes both\.
.P
A command line the tool will not take is reported as that one \fBerror:\fR line on standard error, with no usage text after it: the line describes the problem, and \fB\-h\fR prints the usage text for a reader who wants it\. An option the tool does not know reads \fBerror: unrecognized option '\-Z'\fR; one whose value is missing reads \fBerror: option '\-A' requires a value\fR\.
.P
\fB\-h\fR is what the user asked for, so the usage text goes to standard output\. Nothing else goes to standard error either, unless the rest of the command line produced a warning before \fB\-h\fR ended the command\. A bare \fBubertooth\-btle\fR, with no arguments at all, is the one refusal the usage text does follow, on standard error with the \fBerror:\fR line\.
.SH "EXIT STATUS"
0 when the command did what was asked, including \fB\-h\fR, and after a capture is interrupted (Ctrl\-C) having polled cleanly to that point\.
.P
0 as well for a run stopped by Ctrl\-C before it had opened a board: see ubertooth(7), SELECTING A DEVICE\.
.P
1 for a command line the tool will not take or with nothing to do, for a capture file that cannot be created, when the selected Ubertooth is not found or cannot be opened, when the board refuses the jam mode, a \fB\-t\fR target, a sniffing or faux slave mode, or an access address or CRC verify request, and when a capture stops short because a poll failed\. No device is opened for a refused command line\.
.P
A poll the board does not answer is tried again, up to three attempts of one second each, so one unlucky poll does not end a capture that has been running for a quarter of an hour\. A stop (Ctrl\-C, or SIGTERM, SIGHUP or SIGQUIT) drops the attempts still to come: the attempt already under way finishes, which costs up to a second, and if it failed, that failure is reported (\fBerror: USB poll failed\fR) and the run exits 1, where a capture left to use its remaining attempts might have recovered and exited 0\.
.P
1 as well when the board refuses the request that ends a capture: it is left in the capture mode, and no other tool can open it until it is reset or re\-plugged\. What was captured stays written out, and libubertooth names the refused request on standard error\.
.P
A \fB\-q\fR, \fB\-c\fR or \fB\-r\fR file is created once the board has been opened and its firmware accepted, not before, so a run refused for either reason leaves no empty capture behind\. A file the tool then cannot create is reported with the name the user gave, and the board is given back\.
.P
\fB\-V\fR reads the attached board, so it exits 1 when a board is attached and cannot be read, or when the USB bus cannot be listed at all; see ubertooth(7), COMMON OPTIONS\.
